[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed Caching in .NET: IDistributedCache, Redis, and Cache Aside Patterns"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern cloud architectures, scaling applications horizontally requires externalizing memory storage. In-memory caching (like IMemoryCache) fails when multiple server instances run behind a load balancer because each instance maintains its own isolated cache. Distributed caching solves this by utilizing a centralized cache store like Redis, ensuring consistency across all application nodes."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding IDistributedCache"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":".NET provides the IDistributedCache interface in the Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Abstractions namespace. This interface decouples your application code from specific caching implementations, allowing you to seamlessly switch between Redis, NCache, or SQL Server distributed caches without changing business logic."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Setting up Redis in .NET"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use Redis as your distributed cache provider, install the Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.StackExchangeRedis NuGet package. Then, configure it in your Program.cs file."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"builder.Services.AddStackExchangeRedisCache(options => {
    options.Configuration = builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString("RedisConnection");
    options.InstanceName = "ProductService_";
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Implementing the Cache-Aside Pattern"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Cache-Aside pattern (also known as Lazy Loading) ensures that the cache is loaded only when data is requested. The application first checks the cache; on a cache miss, it queries the database, populates the cache with the retrieved data, and returns it to the client."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public async Task<Product> GetProductAsync(int id)
{
    string cacheKey = "product_" + id;

    // 1. Try to get from cache
    byte[] cachedData = await _distributedCache.GetAsync(cacheKey);

    if (cachedData != null)
    {
        // Cache hit
        string jsonString = Encoding.UTF8.GetString(cachedData);
        return JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Product>(jsonString);
    }

    // 2. Cache miss: Fetch from database
    Product product = await _dbContext.Products.FindAsync(id);
    if (product == null) return null;

    // 3. Store in cache with expiration
    string serializedProduct = JsonSerializer.Serialize(product);
    byte[] dataToCache = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(serializedProduct);

    var options = new DistributedCacheEntryOptions()
        .SetAbsoluteExpiration(TimeSpan.FromMinutes(10))
        .SetSlidingExpiration(TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2));

    await _distributedCache.SetAsync(cacheKey, dataToCache, options);

    return product;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Cache Invalidation Strategies"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Caching introduces the challenge of stale data. Proper cache invalidation is critical when underlying data changes. Whenever a record is updated or deleted, you must explicitly remove or update the corresponding cache entry."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public async Task UpdateProductAsync(Product product)
{
    // 1. Update database
    _dbContext.Products.Update(product);
    await _dbContext.SaveChangesAsync();

    // 2. Invalidate cache
    string cacheKey = "product_" + product.Id;
    await _distributedCache.RemoveAsync(cacheKey);
}"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Absolute Expiration to enforce a hard ceiling on how long stale data can persist."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Sliding Expiration to keep frequently accessed items alive automatically."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Perform proactive invalidation on Write operations (Update/Delete) to avoid race conditions."}]
